(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-general-survey-vitals-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"General Survey & Vital Signs — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"According to the BMI (body mass index) classification table, a BMI (body mass index) of 18.5-24.9 kg/m2 is classified as...","choices":["Underweight","Normal","Obesity Class I","Overweight"],"answer":1,"exp":"A BMI (body mass index) of 18.5-24.9 kg/m2 is classified as normal weight; under 18.5 is underweight, 25.0-29.9 is overweight, and 30 or above is classified into obesity classes I, II, and III (extreme obesity, >40). — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"According to the BMI (body mass index) classification table, 'Extreme obesity' (Obesity Class III) corresponds to a BMI (body mass index) of...","choices":["greater than 40 kg/m2","18.5 to 24.9 kg/m2","25.0 to 29.9 kg/m2","less than 18.5 kg/m2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Extreme obesity (Obesity Class III) is classified as BMI (body mass index) greater than 40 kg/m2, the highest category on the classification table. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"When measuring weight as part of the general survey, proper technique includes having the patient remove shoes/heavy clothing, zeroing/taring the scale, and ensuring the patient is...","choices":["not supported by a wall or holding objects such as a cane","wearing their heaviest coat and boots for extra warmth","leaning firmly against the wall for added stability","holding onto a cane or handrail for balance during weighing"],"answer":0,"exp":"For an accurate weight measurement, the patient should stand unsupported (not leaning on a wall) and without holding assistive objects like a cane, in addition to removing shoes and heavy clothing and using a zeroed/tared scale. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"When measuring height, proper technique includes having the patient stand tall with the head midline, knees straight, and feet...","choices":["in line with the hips","crossed at the ankles","it does not matter how the feet are positioned","wide apart, well beyond hip width"],"answer":0,"exp":"Proper height measurement technique has the patient stand tall with the head midline, knees straight, and feet positioned in line with the hips, measuring to the nearest 1 mm. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"According to the blood pressure classification table, a reading with systolic 125 mmHg and diastolic 75 mmHg would be classified as...","choices":["Elevated","Stage 2 Hypertension","Normal","Stage 1 Hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"A systolic of 120-129 mmHg with a diastolic under 80 mmHg is classified as 'Elevated' blood pressure — higher than normal (systolic under 120 and diastolic under 80) but not yet meeting the threshold for Stage 1 hypertension. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 59"},{"q":"According to the blood pressure classification table, Stage 2 hypertension is defined as a systolic of 140 mmHg or greater, or a diastolic of...","choices":["50 mmHg or lower","there is no diastolic criterion for Stage 2 hypertension","70-79 mmHg","90 mmHg or greater"],"answer":3,"exp":"Stage 2 hypertension is defined as systolic ≥140 mmHg OR diastolic ≥90 mmHg — meeting either threshold alone is sufficient for this classification. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 59"},{"q":"Normal blood pressure, per the classification table, requires a systolic under 120 mmHg and a diastolic...","choices":["there is no diastolic requirement for normal BP (blood pressure)","between 80-89 mmHg","under 80 mmHg","over 90 mmHg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Normal blood pressure is defined as systolic under 120 mmHg AND diastolic under 80 mmHg — both criteria must be met for a 'Normal' classification. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 59"},{"q":"During cuff deflation for BP (blood pressure) measurement, the recommended deflation rate while listening for Korotkoff sounds is approximately...","choices":["there is no recommended deflation rate","0.1 mmHg per second","50 mmHg per second","2-3 mmHg per second"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cuff should be deflated slowly, at a rate of approximately 2-3 mmHg per second, while listening for Korotkoff sounds — deflating too quickly risks missing the precise systolic/diastolic transition points. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 56"},{"q":"If blood pressure readings differ by more than 5 mmHg between the two arms, the recommended approach is to...","choices":["immediately refer to a cardiologist without repeating","only ever measure BP (blood pressure) on one arm from that point forward","repeat the readings","ignore the discrepancy entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"A minor difference between arms is expected, but if the difference exceeds 5 mmHg, the readings should be repeated to confirm accuracy, waiting at least 1 minute between measurements and averaging the results. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 58"},{"q":"When repeating blood pressure readings, the examiner should wait at least how long between measurements, and should expect that...","choices":["1 second; both readings will always be identical","there being no recommended wait time or expected pattern","24 hours; the last reading will usually be the highest","1 minute; the first reading will usually be the highest"],"answer":3,"exp":"At least 1 minute should elapse between repeated BP (blood pressure) measurements, and it's expected that the first reading will usually be the highest (readings tend to decrease slightly with repeated measurement as the patient relaxes), with the readings then averaged. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 58"},{"q":"Improper technique effects on blood pressure include an unsupported arm, crossed legs, an arm positioned below heart level, and a talking patient, all of which produce a reading that is...","choices":["artificially low","artificially high","only affecting heart rate, not blood pressure","unaffected by any of these factors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each of these technique errors — an unsupported arm, crossed legs, an arm below heart level, or a talking patient — is specifically listed as producing an artificially high blood pressure reading. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"Slow or repetitive cuff inflation during BP (blood pressure) measurement can cause inaccurate readings due to...","choices":["increased patient comfort","venous congestion","no effect on reading accuracy","improved arterial flow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Slow or repeatedly inflating and deflating the cuff can cause venous congestion in the arm, which distorts the pressure readings and leads to inaccuracy. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"Among the factors that can transiently elevate blood pressure readings are pain, anxiety, a full bladder, recent exercise, and...","choices":["cold temperature/chilling","recent alcohol consumption","having recently eaten a meal","significant volume depletion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cold temperature or chilling is listed alongside pain, anxiety, a full bladder, recent exercise, sleep deprivation, and recent smoking/caffeine intake as a factor that can transiently elevate blood pressure readings. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 61"},{"q":"Among the factors that can transiently lower blood pressure readings are recent eating, alcohol, volume depletion, and...","choices":["acute severe pain","situational anxiety","autonomic failure","recent tobacco use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Autonomic failure (which can impair normal vasoconstrictive compensation) is listed alongside recent eating, alcohol, and volume depletion as a factor that can lower blood pressure, along with conditions like multiple system atrophy causing orthostatic hypotension. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 62"},{"q":"Orthostatic vital sign measurement ('tilts') involves measuring heart rate and blood pressure in which sequence of positions?","choices":["Standing, sitting, then supine only","Only standing, with no other position","Prone, then supine only","Supine, sitting, then standing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Orthostatic vitals are measured sequentially in the supine, sitting, and then standing positions, evaluated immediately and again at one and three minutes after each position change. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"Orthostatic hypotension is defined by specific changes upon standing, including a pulse rise greater than 10-20 bpm, and a fall of systolic blood pressure greater than...","choices":["2 mmHg","there is no systolic threshold for orthostatic hypotension","20 mmHg","100 mmHg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Orthostatic hypotension is defined by a pulse rise of more than 10-20 bpm, a systolic BP (blood pressure) fall of more than 20 mmHg, and/or a diastolic BP (blood pressure) fall of more than 10 mmHg upon standing. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"Normal orthostatic variation (not meeting criteria for orthostatic hypotension) can include up to a 10 bpm rise in heart rate and a systolic pressure change of approximately...","choices":["there is no defined range for normal orthostatic variation","0 mmHg change only, with zero tolerance","100 mmHg rise or fall","10-15 mmHg rise or fall"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal orthostatic variation can include up to a 10 bpm heart rate rise and a systolic pressure change of about 10-15 mmHg (rise or fall), along with up to a 5 mmHg diastolic change — all considered within normal limits, distinct from the more pronounced changes defining orthostatic hypotension. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"Pulse oximetry works by passing small beams of light through the skin to the blood and estimating oxygen saturation based on...","choices":["differences in light absorption between oxygenated and deoxygenated blood","measuring the surface temperature of the skin at the probe site","direct laboratory chemical analysis of a small sample of drawn blood","the reflection of high-frequency sound waves off blood cells, like ultrasound"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pulse oximetry estimates blood oxygen saturation by measuring differences in how oxygenated versus deoxygenated hemoglobin absorb specific wavelengths of light passed through the tissue. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 64"},{"q":"A pulse oximetry sensor can typically be placed on the...","choices":["only the abdomen","finger, earlobe, or toe","only the forehead, with no other sites possible","nowhere on the body; it requires a blood draw"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pulse oximetry sensors are commonly placed on the finger, earlobe, or toe — sites with adequate blood flow and thin enough tissue for the light-based measurement to work effectively. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 64"},{"q":"A regular pulse rate greater than 100 bpm (sinus tachycardia) can be caused by fever, anemia, dehydration, hyperthyroidism, or...","choices":["certain drugs","being a highly trained athlete","hypothyroidism","third-degree heart block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Among the listed potential causes of sinus tachycardia (regular pulse >100 bpm) are fever, anemia, dehydration, hyperthyroidism, and certain drugs, reflecting a range of physiologic states that can increase heart rate. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"A regular pulse rate less than 60 bpm (sinus bradycardia or low heart block) can be seen in trained athletes, hypothyroidism, or...","choices":["untreated hyperthyroidism","significant dehydration","third-degree heart block","an acute febrile illness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Third-degree (complete) heart block is listed alongside athletic conditioning and hypothyroidism as a cause of a slow, regular pulse rate (bradycardia). — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"An 'irregularly irregular' pulse pattern, as distinguished from a 'regularly irregular' pattern, raises particular concern for...","choices":["ventricular bigeminy or trigeminy specifically, occurring in a fixed repeating pattern","Mobitz type I second-degree AV block specifically, and no other rhythm","a completely normal finding that requires no further cardiac evaluation","atrial fibrillation or frequent premature ventricular contractions (PVCs)"],"answer":3,"exp":"An irregularly irregular pulse (with no predictable pattern to the irregularity) raises concern for atrial fibrillation or frequent PVCs, in contrast to a regularly irregular pattern (a predictable repeating irregularity), which suggests something like bigeminy/trigeminy or Mobitz I AV (atrioventricular) block. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 41"},{"q":"Pulse quality/character is described using terms including weak, full, and...","choices":["bounding","hyperactive","tympanic","vesicular"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pulse quality (character/intensity) is described using terms such as weak, full, or bounding, reflecting the perceived strength and volume of the pulsation on palpation. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 42"},{"q":"Tachypnea refers to abnormally fast breathing, while bradypnea refers to...","choices":["abnormally slow breathing","exaggerated, deep, rapid, or labored breathing","abnormally fast breathing, identical to tachypnea","temporary cessation of breathing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Bradypnea specifically refers to abnormally slow breathing, the opposite of tachypnea (abnormally fast breathing). — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"Hyperpnea, described as exaggerated deep, rapid, or labored breathing, most commonly occurs after exercise but could also occur with...","choices":["a completely normal, resting state with no exertion or illness","hypothyroidism exclusively","aspirin overdose","deep sleep"],"answer":2,"exp":"Beyond its common occurrence after exercise, hyperpnea is specifically noted as a possible finding with aspirin (salicylate) overdose, which can stimulate the respiratory center. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"Apnea is defined as...","choices":["a temporary cessation of breathing","permanently normal breathing with no interruption","abnormally fast breathing","deep, labored breathing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Apnea refers to a temporary cessation of breathing, distinct from bradypnea (slow but continuing breathing) or hypopnea (shallow/infrequent/slow breathing). — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"Pyrexia (fever) can result from infection as well as several non-infectious causes, including ovulation, thyroid storm, drug effects, and...","choices":["only viral infections, never bacterial","environmental factors","hypothyroidism exclusively","nothing besides infection causes fever"],"answer":1,"exp":"Beyond infection, pyrexia can arise from non-infectious causes including ovulation, thyroid storm, iatrogenic drug effects, and environmental factors (for example, heat exposure). — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"To convert a Celsius temperature to Fahrenheit, the formula used is Tf = (9/5) × Tc + 32; using this formula, a temperature of 38°C converts to approximately...","choices":["70.0°F","38.0°F","212.0°F","100.4°F"],"answer":3,"exp":"Applying Tf = (9/5) × 38 + 32 gives approximately 100.4°F, a temperature considered close to febrile range, compared with normal body temperature of 37°C (98.6°F). — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 31"},{"q":"When introducing oneself to a patient at the start of an encounter, the two identifying factors specifically recommended for confirming patient identity are the patient's name and...","choices":["insurance ID number","social security number","home address only","date of birth"],"answer":3,"exp":"The two recommended identifying factors for confirming patient identity are the patient's name and date of birth, standard practice for accurate identification before providing care. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Constitutional symptoms, screened for early in the history, commonly include fatigue, weakness, fever/chills/night sweats, and...","choices":["visual acuity change (better versus worse)","weight change (intentional versus unintentional)","nothing beyond fatigue is ever screened for","symptoms limited to only a single organ system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Constitutional symptoms — which broadly affect overall health, vitality, and strength — commonly include fatigue, weakness, fever/chills/night sweats, and weight change, with intentional versus unintentional weight change being an important distinction to clarify. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 6"}]};
